# Line endings are settled HERE rather than by each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# The INDEX was never the problem: 1188 of 1190 tracked files were already LF (the two
# exceptions were auto-detected binaries). The split was created at CHECKOUT — Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so the same commit materialized as CRLF on a
# Windows working tree and LF on Linux.
#
# That split broke CI run 30320616634. A C# raw string literal keeps its SOURCE FILE's
# line endings, so the "\n"-anchored string surgery in TestArtifactDerivationTests
# silently matched nothing on the Windows runner while passing on the Ubuntu portability
# probe. The test was made line-ending agnostic, but the underlying platform discrepancy
# is what this file removes.
#
# `eol=lf` is the operative part, NOT a bare `text=auto`. A bare `text=auto` normalizes
# the INDEX — which was already clean — and leaves the Windows/Linux WORKING-TREE split
# exactly where it was, so it would not have prevented the failure above. `eol=lf` pins
# the working tree to LF on every platform, making checkouts byte-identical everywhere.
#
# `text=auto` is still present so Git keeps running its binary detection: genuinely
# binary content is never line-ending converted. This repo has no .bat/.cmd files, which
# are the usual reason to carve out a CRLF exception.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Patches encode their own payload's line endings; rewriting them would corrupt the diff
# they carry. Frozen verbatim, which is also how Git already treated this one.
*.patch -text

# Defensive binary guards. Nothing matching these is tracked today, but an image or font
# added later must never be line-ending converted on the strength of a bad text sniff.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.zip binary
*.vsix binary
